The failures now show the reported mechanism, so I'm marking them as relevant.

**Root cause (1–3).** The earlier rounds only showed `TooManyEvaluationsException`, which could have come from slow convergence. This version records every function evaluation and measures the longest run on one side of the root. Both failures show 98 consecutive evaluations with the same sign of f, out of 100 total. That is the reported pattern: one bracket end is updated every iteration while the other never changes. It also fits the report's explanation that the aging target is too close to zero, so the interpolation always lands on the same side. The solver then hits the evaluation limit, which is the title symptom. After a fix that rebalances the bracket, the solver should alternate sides and converge, so both assertions would pass.

**`buggyIf` (4, 5).** It is still a hand-picked box, but the failures are consistent across it. Both failures lie inside the box (scale 9.8e8 and 1.04e9, eps 5.8e-8 and 6.9e-8) and show the same one-sided pattern. Across all rounds, the failures cover scale 9.07e8 to 1.07e9, eps 5.3e-8 to 1.8e-7, max 25.9 to 34.2, start 10.5 to 19.2, and `AllowedSolution` indices 0 to 4. No counter-example inside the box has been shown, so I can't show it is exact. The other half of the generated inputs are drawn from a much wider range, and none of those have been reported as failing.

**Valid domain (6).** The generators span a wide range of scale, eps, max, start and all five `AllowedSolution` values. They don't cover arbitrary functions, but a single analytic test function is a reasonable fixture for a property test like this.

The remaining uncertainty is how exact the box is, not whether the test reproduces the bug.

